## Quick comparison

| Operator | Returns | Duplicates | Order of queries matters? |
|---|---|---|---|
| `UNION` | All distinct rows from both queries | Removed | No |
| `UNION ALL` | All rows from both queries | Kept | No |
| `EXCEPT` (`MINUS` in Oracle) | Distinct rows from the first query that are not in the second | Removed | **Yes** |
| `INTERSECT` | Only the rows found in both queries | Removed | No |

> `UNION ALL` is faster than `UNION` because it skips the step of finding and removing duplicates.


